<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">K'iche' people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Quiché</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The K'iche' are a Maya people of the western highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of El Quiché, Totonicapán and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzaltenango">Quetzaltenango</a>. In the 15th century their kingdom, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qumarkaj">Q'umarkaj</a>, dominated much of the highlands until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> conquered it in 1524. Their sacred book, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh</a>, survives, and K'iche' is the most widely spoken Maya language today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kiche-people.jpg" alt="K'iche' people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bruno Rijsman · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chichicastenango_Market_(Guatemala,_March_2020)_-_2_women.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…generally believed that Ximénez worked from a phonetic manuscript borrowed from a parishioner, a rendering of an oral recitation given in or around Santa Cruz del <b>Quiché</b> soon after Pedro de Alvarado's conquest of 1524; yet, as Néstor Quiroa points out, no such manuscript has ever been found.</blockquote><a href="/work/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom fought the <b>K'iche'</b> and Tz'utujil kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/state/kaqchikel-kingdom">Kaqchikel kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>According to their own chronicles, the <b>K'iche'</b> descended from warrior lineages that arrived in the highlands from the east, probably from the Gulf Coast region, and conquered local peoples in the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kiche-kingdom">K'iche' kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Postclassic, from around 1250, it was the capital of a Mam kingdom that controlled much of the western highlands until <b>K'iche'</b> expansion pushed the Mam back.</blockquote><a href="/state/mam-kingdom">Mam kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Los Altos was a liberal government led by men such as Marcelo Molina, ruling a region with a large <b>K'iche'</b> and Mam Maya majority who had little say.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-los-altos">State of Los Altos</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tz'utujil were one of the highland Maya peoples whose lineages, like those of the <b>K'iche'</b>, claimed origins in migrations from the east.</blockquote><a href="/state/tzutujil-kingdom">Tz'utujil kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
